WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.900
- I believe that right
there is the definition

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.810
of entrepreneurship, it's
when you love your game

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.143
more than what the game gives you.

00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:11.780
Ay!

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.429
You got your perspective.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:15.679
(cheering)

00:00:16.911 --> 00:00:18.773
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:21.440
(hip hop music)

00:00:22.697 --> 00:00:24.947
(cheering)

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:28.263
- Gary V.

00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:30.743
- Oh ho, the Darnold
jersey, what's up Jet fans?

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:34.050
Darnold and Leonard, I love it.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.020
I feel warm already.
- [Man] I love you Gary!

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.711
- I love you back mom.

00:00:37.711 --> 00:00:39.110
(laughing)

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.510
- Exactly, everyone thinks
that they already know

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.570
you and think they can just
talk to you whenever they want.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.453
- They can, I'm like, I love you back.

00:00:48.668 --> 00:00:50.610
I think social came along,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.170
the internet evolution came along.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.490
at a very good, life is
so much about timing.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.700
I was born in the former Soviet Union,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.692
and if I was my grandfather,

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:02.300
I'd probably be in a Soviet jail

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.260
during this time of my
life and I'm not kidding

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.090
because both my grandfathers
spent time in a Soviet jail.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.960
I happen to have the serendipity of timing

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.190
where the internet came
along and created a framework

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.790
that allows people to interact at scale.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.860
I disproportionately enjoy human beings.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.083
I actually secretly dislike animals.

00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:23.350
(laughing)

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.440
Because so many people like
animals more than other humans

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.690
and it always breaks my
head so they can come

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.323
up to me, I like being accessible.

00:01:33.841 --> 00:01:37.260
I like saying hello, I love
the idea of that one moment

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.670
in time that I get to meet
somebody and so I'm super

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.133
interested in meeting
all 7.8 billion people.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.167
And so, yeah I feel
that way about them too.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.190
- That's good, I mean, you can tell.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:52.950
And we're gonna talk a
lot about authenticity

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.180
and knowing who you are.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.660
You came four years ago,
sort of blew into my class,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.040
sat up there, no questions.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.570
Some people just get out
of the way because you just

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.040
know that they're gonna do it without you.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.200
You gave a 45 minute talk, it
felt like it was off the cuff.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.360
It was so powerful, I mean
that really was a great day.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.140
I didn't even think it was
here, it was really spectacular.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.860
Years later you gave this
credit for helping blow

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.174
up your Facebook platform?

00:02:20.174 --> 00:02:21.007
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:21.850
- Is that really a good talk?

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.040
- That talk, I was telling
some people right before,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.824
thank you for that invite.

00:02:26.824 --> 00:02:27.860
It was definitely off the cuff,

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.883
I'm only comfortable in improv.

00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:34.320
I stay in my lane, I talk about
what I know and don't talk

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.280
about what I don't know and
that makes everything very

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.683
easy and off the cuff.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.940
It was an interesting time because

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.913
four years ago, again, back to timing.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.210
When I walked into that
classroom it was clearly

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.970
the time where something
had transpired in me which

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.500
was a concern that entrepreneurship
was about to become rock

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.163
star status and if you
listen to that talk,

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.323
I'm coming aggressively but
it's out of love not disrespect.

00:03:06.296 --> 00:03:09.520
At that point and way more
so today, I have a concern

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.740
that we're living through the greatest

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.590
era of fake entrepreneurship.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.020
I believe that self awareness
is incredibly important

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.420
and I do believe that the
coolness of entrepreneurship

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.150
has made people go towards
that light that might actually

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:25.780
be way better being a
number three or number six

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.107
or number nine and so.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.480
The opening line in the class

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.290
was I don't think
entrepreneurship can be taught.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.350
And where I was going with that,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.963
- So that's awkward he says.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.750
- And where I'm going with
that is actually, I've refined

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.500
that message 'cause it was the
first time I've ever said it.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.680
I think of entrepreneurship so much more

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.620
similarly to sports.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.070
I could be better at basketball, right?

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.900
I could play everyday, I
could work on my left hand,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.760
I could do things and I
would be substantially better

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.080
in a year and a half.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.170
I just never had a shot
of going to the league.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.130
And I think that there's
a big difference between

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.390
being an entrepreneurship and being

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.003
a successful entrepreneur.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.770
That's a conversation
that I subtly found myself

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.080
bringing to the forefront four
years ago in your classroom

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.970
and then what happened was,
we got serious about Facebook

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.180
on my end and we chopped
up that key note into a lot

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.520
of different points and many
of the clips from that talk

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.760
went on to get
disproportionate virality so.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.620
You were great, the team
around you was great,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.622
the campus was great, the
vibe was great, it was just

00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:37.622
all good stuff and I'm really
really happy to be here again.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:40.600
- It's great to have you back.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.480
It is sort of back by popular demand.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.350
We have a series every Wednesday
and so I ask the students

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:46.430
midway through the semester,

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.110
if you have anybody who would it be?

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:49.660
And they started naming
some people and then all

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:50.790
of a sudden you felt this vibe.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.870
We want an influencer,
we want social media,

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.703
we want some of those people,

00:04:53.703 --> 00:04:55.190
and then someone yelled Gary
Vaynerchuk and the whole

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.920
class just starts going yes, yes, yes.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.300
And at the time, sorta we
started talkin' about it.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.900
So like it might happen,
maybe it'll happen

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.020
and then when it did they
really were pretty pumped

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.630
so there're a lot of students
missing other classes

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.290
tonight to be here.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.969
- Best decision of your career.

00:05:09.969 --> 00:05:13.148
(laughing and applauding)

00:05:13.148 --> 00:05:13.981
It is.

00:05:15.718 --> 00:05:17.802
I'm very confident about that.

00:05:17.802 --> 00:05:19.833
- We're gonna get into
education in a minute

00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:23.590
but first, the thing that
struck me with so much clarity

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:25.700
and you know sometimes you
hear something and it's just

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.570
the way someone said a little differently.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.840
And it really was your
message around self awareness

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:31.950
and knowing your strengths.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.940
Could you sort of, not
everybody heard that,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.470
can you tell us what you mean by that?

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.100
And why you said it's
the most important thing

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.100
for an entrepreneur.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.150
- I think of it in practicality.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:47.300
I don't think you get the
same returns by overwhelmingly

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.890
working on your weaknesses
as you do on tripling down

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.440
on your strengths.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.274
I don't know what else to say.

00:05:54.274 --> 00:05:59.274
It's so clear to me, I think
so much of what I talk about

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.121
is predicated on not only
my successes but watching

00:06:03.121 --> 00:06:06.390
my family members, watching
that whole ecosystem

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.460
of startup founders, just watching.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.500
I watch a lot, I read a
lot of people's behavior

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.730
more so than books or
things of that nature

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:21.020
and so yeah I mean, I believe
that the entrepreneurs

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.580
that have gone on to quadruple
down on their strengths

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.250
and then hire around their
weaknesses have had much

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.870
better success than the
ones that dwell on their

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.440
shortcomings because somebody
they look up to was good

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.840
at it and said it was
important and they waste their

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.590
time on something they'll never be.

00:06:38.859 --> 00:06:42.740
- You could spend decades of
your life trying to improve

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:44.780
on some of those weaker things
when really if you really

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.360
just understood what makes
you unique, and what makes

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.760
you different, and better,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.330
there's some things you're just better at.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.020
And if you keep practicing
you get even better.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.063
- And almost everything is a commodity.

00:06:57.238 --> 00:06:59.890
Most things are commodities.

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.240
Almost nothing...

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:02.412
In the scheme of things
there're very few things

00:07:02.412 --> 00:07:05.760
that matter and most of
the things that actually

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.960
matter are predicated on
emotional intelligence,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.160
not on information you know and deploy.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.380
So, you know, I don't know.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.960
And then you get into other
things like when you talk

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.930
about the current state of
startups and venture capital

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:24.140
and the whole thing, we're
living in the greatest era

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:29.030
of financial arbitrage
machines, not actual businesses.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:32.430
Everybody's life right
now is about CAC and LTV

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.330
and hitting metrics to get
the next fundraising round

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.660
and has nothing to do
with the end consumer.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.850
It's B2B finance game on
the back end and that's

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.060
why so many people are gonna lose.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.597
- And yet you still think
it's a great time to be

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:47.072
and entrepreneur in terms of
the ability to raise money.

00:07:47.072 --> 00:07:52.072
- To me it's not the last
part but yes, raising capital

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:56.320
is a piece of cake today at a
level that we've never seen.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.050
It's ludicrous, the
youngsters in this audience

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:02.810
think it's normal to get
somebody to give you money

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.120
based on your idea at
a four million dollar

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.803
or six million dollar evaluation.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.174
That makes me want to
punch you in the face.

00:08:11.174 --> 00:08:12.850
(laughing)

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:14.800
Because for anybody in here who's over 40,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.590
that is a ludicrous concept.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.660
But that is what happens
when there's an enormous

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.130
amount of money pumped into the system,

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.579
on the back of us not paying
the piper properly in 2009

00:08:25.579 --> 00:08:27.580
'cause we're soft as a culture.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.270
And so, this is a very fake
entrepreneur environment.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.520
When I see entrepreneurs
that are failing in this

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.870
environment with how much
capital, with how little it costs

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.963
to be in the game, with
the internet at full scale.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.750
If you're an entrepreneur
that's failing right now

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.761
that's a year or two in, you suck.

00:08:47.761 --> 00:08:49.840
(laughing)

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.710
I mean it.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.140
You suck at entrepreneurship.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.513
Doesn't mean you suck.

00:08:54.884 --> 00:08:56.593
I suck at piano.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:02.560
But let there be no mistake,
if you're 18-24 months

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.360
operating business in this
environment, and you're not

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.596
winning with all those advantages,

00:09:07.596 --> 00:09:09.040
(clicks tongue)

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:10.083
you're not an A.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:13.500
- I think it's important
'cause you don't have

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.723
the perspective of history for
people who are 20 years old.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.710
You're a lucky time where you
can raise a half a million

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.460
dollars on a okay idea.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.350
What do kids their age in
2008 when they graduated,

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:25.850
what did they have to do?

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:26.780
- Not do that.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.330
- [David] Yeah, they'd go get a job.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:29.163
- Correct.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.240
- Yeah, so it's a unique
time to be an entrepreneur.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.073
You referenced it.

00:09:34.073 --> 00:09:38.110
- Which gets into
entitlement and being soft,

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:41.007
and then people think
their ideas are good.

00:09:41.007 --> 00:09:42.340
It gets really convoluted.

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:43.583
We need a correction.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.110
You're talking about a person
who's disproportionately

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:51.630
benefited by the cool
factor of entrepreneurship

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:52.550
but I don't want that.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.223
I appreciate it, as you can imagine,

00:09:55.223 --> 00:09:57.850
coming on stage and getting
this kind of love from

00:09:57.850 --> 00:09:59.530
the 20 year old said, it's
like something I could've

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.946
never dreamed of, it feels remarkable.

00:10:01.946 --> 00:10:05.310
But I'm unbelievably passionate
about over-communicating

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.010
the shortcomings of an
environment where entrepreneurship

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:11.680
is cool, it's the same way
that being a professional

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.600
athlete and a rapper is cool.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.150
Very few people can actually
achieve it at a heavy,

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:18.080
high level success.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.970
So two things are important to me.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:23.168
One, I want to redefine what
a successful entrepreneurship

00:10:23.168 --> 00:10:25.150
path looks like.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:29.560
If you make a $136,000 a year,
on a business that you run

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.020
that you like doing
around something you like,

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:34.790
and you can live that
lifestyle, that is a remarkable

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.600
feat but that is not the
picture we're painting

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.250
of a successful entrepreneur.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.850
We're painting private jets
and islands and all sorts

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.480
of fucking ludicrous shit.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.570
And so, A, I think it's remarkable.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:49.970
We need a level set.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:52.730
The one percent earners in our
country, one of the richest

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.090
countries in the world, the
bottom of the one percent

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.010
make $420,000 a year.

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:00.840
Yet, everybody starts the
conversation of macro big success

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.083
at a million bucks.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:07.810
We're twisted and we also
have to talk way more about

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:10.650
happiness, we also have to
talk about what it looks

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.490
like to have a Scarlet
Letter of an L when you're

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:16.740
company fails in a world where
you've never had a public

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.330
loss in your life because you played

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.060
through a school system.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.860
There's a lot of things
that we have not addressed.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.710
There're a lot of people
right now that are flossing

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:26.180
on Instagram as entrepreneurs
who are gonna to go into

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.380
deep depression or even suicide
when they take a massive

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.350
L on the next correction in our economy,

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:34.530
and that's going to be tough.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.700
- It is a sort of pretty deep
things to start with but yeah,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:38.893
if you are.

00:11:38.893 --> 00:11:41.810
(audience laughs).

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:45.438
Gary dives right in.

00:11:45.438 --> 00:11:48.271
(audience cheers)

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.300
In talking about mindset
you're hitting, we have one

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.710
of the leading researchers
on purposed based identity

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:59.050
as opposed to achievement
based identity where people

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:03.570
tie their self-esteem and
their life to how results

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.040
and success, spoiler alert,
you're not gonna be happy.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.720
And so finding a purpose
aligning with your identity

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.010
that you're gonna lead much better life,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.460
you're gonna be productive
over a longer term

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.210
and you're probably not
gonna hit those low of lows

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.220
that you talked about.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.460
I want to step back really
quickly because you mentioned

